WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.741
Multiplying is counting equal groups.
An array lays them out in neat rows.

2
00:00:09.961 --> 00:00:14.419
See one times fact, and you can
share it out and find its rule too.

3
00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:17.659
Here are four rows, with six in every row.

4
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:22.244
You could count all twenty-four,
one at a time. You do not need to.

5
00:00:22.464 --> 00:00:26.690
Four sixes is twenty-four.
Read it as rows times columns.

6
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.904
Every row must be equal. A
short row is not a times fact.

7
00:00:31.124 --> 00:00:33.446
Now turn the whole array on its side.

8
00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:37.242
Six rows of four. The same
dots, looked at another way.

9
00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:40.203
One array holds four number sentences.

10
00:00:40.423 --> 00:00:45.346
Four times six is twenty-four.
Six times four is twenty-four.

11
00:00:45.566 --> 00:00:48.213
Twenty-four shared between four is six.

12
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:51.359
And twenty-four shared
between six is four.

13
00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.340
When a division will not come,
ask what you multiply by.

14
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.181
Twenty-four times three is big. Split
the twenty-four into twenty and four.

15
00:01:01.401 --> 00:01:04.559
Twenty threes are sixty.
Four threes are twelve.

16
00:01:04.779 --> 00:01:09.423
Sixty and twelve make seventy-two.
Both parts had to be multiplied.

17
00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:15.448
Estimate first. Thirty-eight is nearly forty,
and forty fours are one hundred and sixty.

18
00:01:15.668 --> 00:01:20.033
The real answer is one hundred and
fifty-two. Close, as it should be.

19
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:25.502
Estimate first. Twelve is close to
thirteen, so expect about three each.

20
00:01:25.722 --> 00:01:31.016
Share thirteen sweets between four
friends. Deal them out, one to each.

21
00:01:31.236 --> 00:01:33.790
Three each, and that is twelve given out.

22
00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.215
One sweet is left over.
That one is the remainder.

23
00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:42.265
A remainder must be smaller than the
number of groups. One is smaller than four.

24
00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:47.176
Count up from zero in equal
steps. Five, ten, fifteen, twenty.

25
00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:50.601
Every number you land on
is a multiple of five.

26
00:01:50.821 --> 00:01:55.697
The last digit tells you. Multiples
of five end in nought or five.

27
00:01:55.917 --> 00:02:02.326
Multiples of ten end in nought. Multiples of
two end in nought, two, four, six or eight.

28
00:02:02.826 --> 00:02:08.306
Three, six, nine, twelve. A sequence
goes up by the same amount each time.

29
00:02:08.526 --> 00:02:12.241
To find the rule, take one
term away from the next.

30
00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:16.222
Nine take away six is three.
The rule is add three.

31
00:02:16.442 --> 00:02:20.065
So the next number is fifteen,
and you did not draw it.

32
00:02:20.285 --> 00:02:24.836
Squares can grow by a rule too.
Three, six, nine, then twelve.

33
00:02:25.056 --> 00:02:31.279
Patterns can shrink too. Twelve, nine,
six, then three: take away three each time.

34
00:02:31.779 --> 00:02:36.144
An array shows a times fact, and
turning it round keeps the total.

35
00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:41.008
One array gives four facts, and a
big number can be split into parts.

36
00:02:41.228 --> 00:02:46.197
Sharing can leave a remainder, and
equal steps find multiples and rules.
